{"say":"How do you compare a score to a score from a totally different test? Here's the trick. Convert both into z-scores. A z-score tells you how many standard deviations a score sits above or below the mean.","visualIntent":"Two mismatched bell curves with different scales slide onto one shared axis labeled in standard deviations; the term z-score highlights.","passages":["p1"]},
{"say":"First, meet the standard normal distribution. Its mean is 0. Its standard deviation is 1. That makes it the common ruler everything else gets measured against.","visualIntent":"A single bell curve with the center marked 0 and tick marks at one standard deviation steps; a ruler graphic overlays it.","passages":["p1"]},
{"say":"Here's the key move. The formula is z equals x minus mu, all divided by sigma. Flip it around and you get x equals mu plus z times sigma. One formula takes you to a z-score, the other brings you back to the original score.","visualIntent":"Formula z = (x - mu) / sigma appears, then animates by rearranging into x = mu + z * sigma, with arrows showing the two directions.","passages":["p2"]},
{"say":"Now an example. The mean is 5 and the standard deviation is 2. A score of 11 has a z-score of 3. Above the mean gives a positive z-score. Below the mean gives a negative one. Right on the mean gives zero. So the takeaway is this: a z-score counts standard deviations from the mean, and its sign shows which side you're on.","visualIntent":"Number line with mean 5 marked and steps of 2 up to 11, labeled z equals 3. Then a positive zone, a negative zone and a zero at the mean light up in turn.","passages":["p3"]}
]
